WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.175
Here is the benchmark almost everyone writes first.

2
00:00:06.175 --> 00:00:11.695
Read the clock, call Math.sqrt a hundred million times, and read the clock again.

3
00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:13.784
Each result is thrown away.

4
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:15.990
It runs five rounds.

5
00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:20.566
Round one printed 0.0301 nanoseconds per call.

6
00:00:20.566 --> 00:00:22.356
Round two, 0.1299.

7
00:00:22.356 --> 00:00:25.440
Every later round printed zero.

8
00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:28.495
No CPU takes a square root in zero time.

9
00:00:28.495 --> 00:00:32.838
A second loop adds each result to a sum, and prints the sum.

10
00:00:32.838 --> 00:00:36.095
It settles at about 1.9 nanoseconds per call.

11
00:00:37.667 --> 00:00:40.905
While the program runs, C2 compiles the hot loop.

12
00:00:40.905 --> 00:00:44.869
It looks at the call to sqrt, and asks who reads the result.

13
00:00:45.067 --> 00:00:45.995
Nobody does.

14
00:00:45.995 --> 00:00:48.859
Work whose result nobody uses can go.

15
00:00:48.859 --> 00:00:51.103
That's dead-code elimination.

16
00:00:51.267 --> 00:00:54.758
Now the loop has an empty body, so the loop can go too.

17
00:00:54.758 --> 00:00:57.424
Between the two clock reads, nothing runs.

18
00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:00.636
So the loop measured the optimizer, not your code.

19
00:01:01.467 --> 00:01:03.947
Constant inputs are the second trap.

20
00:01:03.947 --> 00:01:08.563
Say the input is a static final constant, or a const val in Kotlin.

21
00:01:08.767 --> 00:01:11.516
The compiler copies the 2.0 into the call.

22
00:01:11.516 --> 00:01:17.079
Then C2 computes the square root while it compiles, and puts the answer in its place.

23
00:01:17.267 --> 00:01:20.333
Your benchmark now times the load of one number.

24
00:01:20.333 --> 00:01:21.867
That's constant folding.

25
00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:26.935
A broken loop can also print a number that looks fine.

26
00:01:26.935 --> 00:01:34.777
With Math.log instead of sqrt, C2 on JDK 21 kept the call, and the loop reported 7 to 9 nanoseconds.

27
00:01:34.777 --> 00:01:38.933
It still measures an accident of how C2 compiles log.

28
00:01:39.733 --> 00:01:44.884
JMH, the OpenJDK benchmark harness, builds the measuring loop for you.

29
00:01:44.884 --> 00:01:48.195
You write one method, and mark it @Benchmark.

30
00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:54.922
At compile time, JMH generates the loop around your method, written to defeat these tricks.

31
00:01:54.922 --> 00:01:59.293
It runs it in fresh JVMs, and throws away the warm-up rounds.

32
00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:02.203
Return the result, and JMH consumes it.

33
00:02:02.203 --> 00:02:07.193
The JIT has to treat a consumed value as used, so the square root stays.

34
00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:10.564
With more than one result, pass each one to a Blackhole.

35
00:02:11.367 --> 00:02:16.771
And read the input from a field that isn't final, so C2 can't fold it into a constant.
